WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.420
Get ready to be wow-ed because Disney has
really hit the ball out of the park with Beauty

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.610
and the Beast, their latest live-action take
on one of their animated classics!

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.610
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.630
Stepping into the role of Belle is Emma Watson,
who gives a lively performance that's an appealing

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.690
blend of warmth, intelligence, and independence.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.390
Watson, of course, already has one iconic
role under her belt in the form of Hermione

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:21.390
in the Harry Potter movies.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:25.430
But by so beautifully updating and bringing
one of Disney's premiere animated princesses

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.340
to life on the big-screen, Watson has ensured
herself another role for which she'll be fondly

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.310
remembered for many years to come.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:35.300
Since making his name in Downton Abbey, Dan
Stevens has been doing a great job avoiding

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.800
posh pigeon holes by keeping his career nice
and varied.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.920
And as the Beast, he puts in a suitably gruff
and witty turn, that's really enjoyable to

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:43.920
watch.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.580
Luke Evans is phenomenal as the uber-arrogant
Gaston; in fact, he's so good it feels like

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:53.400
he was born to play this role, and really
he's the absolute stand-out in the cast for

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.400
me.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.680
Like Josh Gad, who plays LeFou, Evans has
a background in musical theatre, and they

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.880
both get to put that to good use here.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.870
However, unlike Gad, Evans hasn't really ventured
too far before into comedic territory, but

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:09.000
he absolutely crushes it in Beauty and the
Beast, showing he's extremely adept in the

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.150
comedy department.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.940
As LeFou, Josh Gad, who previously gave voice
to Frozen's Olaf, works incredibly well with

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.710
Evans, adding lots of humour and lovely improvised
moments to the film.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.880
Ian McKellen is simply perfect as Cogsworth,
and they may be smaller roles but I love Audra

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.360
McDonald as Garderobe, Stanley Tucci as Cadenza,
and Gugu Mbatha-Raw as Plumette.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.799
Emma Thompson and Ewan McGregor have tough
acts to follow, taking on the roles of Mrs

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:36.590
Potts and Lumiere made famous by Angela Lansbury
and Jerry Orbach.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:41.120
Both put their own spins on the role, adding
some delicious touches, and I enjoyed both

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.130
their performances, though these will still
always be Lansbury and Orbach's roles for

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.130
me.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.570
And while I wasn't especially keen on Nathan
Mack's take on Chip in the trailers, I found

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.510
it worked much better in the actual film,
though I do still prefer Bradley Pierce's

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:54.680
original Chip.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.170
Dreamgirls director Bill Condon, his co-writers
Stephen Chbosky and Evan Spiliotopoulos, and

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.869
cinematographer Tobias Schliessler have put
a delightfully fresh perspective on the classic

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:08.399
animation, while managing to keep alive so
much of what fans love about the original.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:11.900
They've done brilliantly fleshing out many
of the characters, giving them more depth

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.769
and making their actions more believable,
as well as adding more realistic elements

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:19.099
to the plot and ironing out some inconsistencies
in the animated feature.

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:23.389
They've also included a couple of little meta
moments where the film lovingly pokes fun

00:02:23.389 --> 00:02:24.389
at itself.

00:02:24.389 --> 00:02:28.189
And though there's a rare moment that doesn't
feel quite as natural or smoothly executed

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:31.889
as the rest, for instance, when Belle meets
Lumiere, they're so few and far between they're

00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:33.579
of no real consequence.

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:37.890
As for Alan Menken and Howard Ashman's genius
soundtrack from the animation, that plays

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.409
an equally prominent part in this live-action
version.

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:45.459
In fact, it's fantastic to hear such wonderful
new performances of those much-loved tunes,

00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:48.559
including Be Our Guest, Gaston, Belle, and
Beauty and the Beast.

00:02:48.559 --> 00:02:52.529
As well as making a few lyrical and musical
changes to some of the original songs, Menken

00:02:52.529 --> 00:02:56.919
and Tim Rice have also penned three new songs,
my favourite of which is Evermore, sung by

00:02:56.919 --> 00:02:58.310
Dan Stevens.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.879
Speaking of the movie's song and dance numbers,
which are really charming, I've gotta give

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:06.549
props to choreographer Anthony Van Laast for
his work, which channels the animation's influences

00:03:06.549 --> 00:03:10.930
including full-on Busby Berkeley as well as
adding new inspirations, such as a touch of

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.030
7 Brides for 7 Brothers.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.290
Designer Jacqueline Durran is to be congratulated
for her work in the costume department.

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.440
And production designer Sarah Greenwood, Set
Decorator Katie Spencer, and Supervising Art

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.640
Director Niall Moroney have outdone themselves
bringing to life the world of Beauty and the

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.219
Beast, including Belle's village and the Beast's
castle.

00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:32.750
The castle in particular is truly impressive,
with incredibly ornate interiors and exteriors

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:36.620
that seem to grow and subtly change over the
course of the film's two hours.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.900
And, along with the movie's VFX team, they've
done an amazing job creating characters such

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.639
as Lumiere and Cogsworth.

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:46.480
The attention to detail that's gone into the
design of these characters is striking, with

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.939
elements from their human forms influencing
their look.

00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:54.510
My absolute favourite of these is Garderobe,
who is a triumph of imagination.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.849
To sum up: Beauty and the Beast is an enchanting
take on Disney's much-loved animated classic.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:03.459
Director Bill Condon and his cast and crew
have taken what was great about the original

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:07.931
and built on that in new and satisfying ways,
while still including plenty of easter eggs

00:04:07.931 --> 00:04:10.150
and nods to the original for die-hard fans.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.819
The production design and VFX are jaw-dropping
and the costumes, cinematography and song

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:16.790
and dance numbers are gorgeous.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.350
Basically, it's an absolutely magical movie
that put a huge smile on my face right from

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.910
the start yet also managed to bring a tear
to my eye.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.850
And I cannot wait to watch it again!

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:29.760
Now, what are you looking forward to about
the live-action Beauty And The Beast, or if

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.600
you've seen it, what did you think of it?

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.340
Who do you think are going to be your favourite
characters and why?

